	abstract={I apply Schubert's successive h-index framework to OpenAlex, computing h{\$}{\$}{\_}{\{}2{\}}{\$}{\$}for 22,980 institutions and h3 for 214 countries across 13.3 million researchers. The overall institution-level ranking is substantially shaped by biomedical field representation: Medicine accounts for 24{\%} of the author pool and institutions without a large biomedical program are in general structurally disadvantaged, though the overall ranking is not reducible to biomedical representation alone. Field-specific decomposition is more informative: according to this metric, Wageningen University leads Agricultural {\&} Biological Sciences and the University of Chinese Academy of Sciences leads Environmental Science, Materials Science, and Engineering; Carnegie Mellon leads Computer Science from 159th; Brazil holds two of the world's top-six dental-research institutions by field h{\$}{\$}{\_}{\{}2{\}}{\$}{\$}. I normalize for group size using Egghe's Lotkaian scaling and find that Italy and Japan tie on h3 despite a 6{\$}{\$}{\backslash}times{\$}{\$}difference in institutional scale.},
